consiSt("ok",{"courts":[{"l":"HIGHEST COURT","n":"Supreme Court of Oklahoma","d":"Court of last resort in civil cases. Every civil appeal is filed there by petition in error; it keeps some cases and assigns others to the Court of Civil Appeals, and it reviews Court of Civil Appeals decisions only on certiorari granted by a majority of the Justices. It has exclusive authority over bar admission and lawyer discipline (5 O.S. §§ 12-13)."},{"l":"HIGHEST COURT (CRIMINAL)","n":"Court of Criminal Appeals","d":"Separate appellate court for criminal cases; civil appeals do not go there. Its recusal substitution procedure appears in 20 O.S. § 1402(C). Its own rules were not collected."},{"l":"INTERMEDIATE","n":"Court of Civil Appeals","d":"Hears only civil cases assigned by the Supreme Court. At least four permanent three-judge divisions, two in Tulsa County and two in Oklahoma County, plus divisions the Supreme Court convenes, including a Court of Existing Claims Division for pre-2014 workers’ compensation appeals. Opinions are not precedent unless the Supreme Court approves them for publication in the official reporter (20 O.S. §§ 30.1-30.5, 30.14)."},{"l":"TRIAL COURTS","n":"District Courts","d":"General-jurisdiction trial courts organized in 26 judicial districts (20 O.S. § 92.1). Civil procedure is set by statute: the Oklahoma Pleading Code (12 O.S. §§ 2001-2027) and Discovery Code (12 O.S. §§ 3224-3237), supplemented by the Supreme Court’s Rules for District Courts."},{"l":"SPECIALIZED","n":"Business Court Divisions I (Oklahoma County) and II (Tulsa County)","d":"Created within the district courts by 2025 legislation, effective September 1, 2025, for qualifying business and complex cases filed on or after January 1, 2026; damages claims generally need at least $500,000 in controversy, and listed consumer, personal-injury, foreclosure, probate, and similar matters are excluded. Judges are appointed by the Governor with Senate consent; the filing or removal fee is $1,500 (20 O.S. §§ 91.7, 91.7b, 91.7c, 91.7f)."}],"slapp":[{"t":"Statute: Oklahoma Citizens Participation Act","d":"12 O.S. §§ 1430-1440, effective November 1, 2014, with exemptions amended in 2022 and 2025. It protects the rights to free speech (communication on a matter of public concern: health or safety, environmental, economic or community well-being, government, public officials or figures, or marketplace goods and services), to petition (broadly defined to include communications in or about judicial, official, legislative, and agency proceedings and efforts to get government to consider an issue), and of association. It is construed liberally and does not displace other defenses or immunities.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1430, 1431, 1440","v":true},{"t":"Motion to dismiss: 60 days; discovery stops","d":"A party may move to dismiss a legal action (including a counterclaim or cross-claim) that is based on, relates to, or responds to its protected activity. File within 60 days after service of the legal action; the court may extend for good cause. Filing suspends all discovery until the ruling, except specified and limited discovery the court allows for good cause.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1431(6), 1432, 1435(B)","v":true},{"t":"Hearing within 60 days, ruling within 30","d":"The hearing must be set within 60 days after service of the motion, never later than 90 days (120 days if limited discovery is allowed). The court must rule within 30 days after the hearing, considering the pleadings and affidavits. On request of the movant, it must also issue findings within 30 days on whether the suit was brought to deter protected activity or for an improper purpose.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1433, 1434(A), 1435(A), 1436","v":true},{"t":"Burden shifting","d":"The movant must show by a preponderance that the action is based on, relates to, or responds to protected activity. The claimant then avoids dismissal only by establishing by clear and specific evidence a prima facie case for each essential element. Even so, the court must dismiss if the movant proves each element of a valid defense by a preponderance.","c":"12 O.S. § 1434(B)-(D)","v":true},{"t":"Mandatory fees and sanctions","d":"On dismissal, the court must award the movant costs, reasonable attorney fees, and other expenses as justice and equity require, and must sanction the claimant enough to deter similar suits. If the motion is frivolous or solely for delay, the court may award the respondent costs and fees.","c":"12 O.S. § 1438","v":true},{"t":"Appeal: denial by inaction is appealable and expedited","d":"If the court does not rule within the 30 days, the motion is denied by operation of law and the movant may appeal. Appellate courts must expedite appeals and writs from orders on the motion or failure to rule.","c":"12 O.S. § 1437","v":true},{"t":"Exemptions","d":"Does not apply to enforcement actions by the Attorney General or a district attorney; claims against sellers or lessors over statements arising from sales or leases of goods, services, or insurance aimed at actual or potential customers; bodily injury, wrongful death, or survival claims (and statements about them); Insurance Code or insurance-contract claims; and claims from officer-director, employment, or independent-contractor relationships for misappropriating trade secrets or corporate opportunities or enforcing non-disparagement, noncompete, nondisclosure, or confidentiality agreements.","c":"12 O.S. § 1439 (as amended by Laws 2025, c. 180, eff. Nov. 1, 2025)","v":true}],"oos":[{"t":"Pro hac vice: motion with affidavit and Oklahoma associate counsel","d":"Out-of-state counsel’s motion to appear in a district court case must comply with Article II, § 5 of the Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma Bar Association (5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 1). The statement that rule requires must be an affidavit attached to the motion, the motion must show the rule’s requirements are met, and the Oklahoma associate attorney’s entry of appearance must be filed with the motion.","c":"12 O.S. § 2005.2(B)","v":true},{"t":"Article II, § 5 terms (fees, limits, local counsel duties)","d":"The OSCN index lists Art. II § 5 as “Out-of-State Attorneys and Attorneys Granted a Special Temporary Permit To Practice,” but its text — including any application fee, any annual limit, and the associate counsel’s duties — was not collected. Read it on OSCN before filing; no fee or limit is stated here.","c":"Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma Bar Ass’n, art. II, § 5 (5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 1). Not collected: OSCN began serving a Cloudflare Turnstile bot check on November 10, 2026, before this text was downloaded.","v":false},{"t":"Entry of appearance with OBA number is required","d":"Every party must file an entry of appearance (counsel or pro se) no later than its first filing, including the OBA number, mailing address, phone and fax, and firm. Every signed paper must show the signing attorney’s OBA number. Filing the entry of appearance does not waive § 2012(B) defenses.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2005.2(A), 2011(A)","v":true},{"t":"Electronic service only by written consent","d":"E-mail or fax service on a party or counsel is valid only if that person consented in writing in the particular case and gave instructions, which can be done in the entry of appearance. Otherwise serve by hand, mail, or third-party commercial carrier.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2005(B), 2005.2(A)","v":true},{"t":"E-filing is by Supreme Court-approved rule","d":"Fax or electronic filing in the district courts is permitted only as allowed by rules of the Administrative Office of the Courts approved by the Supreme Court, and the Supreme Court is authorized to provide e-filing in the Supreme Court and district courts and must do so in the business courts. The current e-filing rules (District Court Rule 2.1, Supreme Court Rule 1.4) and which counties are live were not collected.","c":"12 O.S. § 2005(E)(3); 20 O.S. §§ 3004, 91.7f(A); Rule 2.1, Rules for District Courts (not collected)","v":false},{"t":"Limited-scope representation","d":"The OSCN index shows District Court Rule 33, “Limited Scope Representation,” and Rule of Professional Conduct 1.2 covers scope of representation, but neither text was collected. Withdrawal under the statute requires leave of court, a proposed order, and either the client’s signature or a certificate of notice.","c":"Rule 33, Rules for District Courts (not collected); 12 O.S. § 2005.2(C)","v":false}],"ethics":[{"t":"Governing code: Oklahoma Rules of Professional Conduct","d":"The Oklahoma Rules of Professional Conduct are at 5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 3-A (cited that way in 12 O.S. § 3226.2), and the Supreme Court has exclusive power to discipline lawyers. The OSCN index shows they follow the ABA Model Rules numbering (Rules 1.0 through 8.5). The rule text was not collected, so no textual differences from the Model Rules are stated here.","c":"5 O.S. § 13; 5 O.S. ch. 1, app. 3-A. Not collected: OSCN began serving a Cloudflare Turnstile bot check on November 10, 2026, before this text was downloaded.","v":false},{"t":"Statutory duties of attorneys still on the books","d":"A 1910 statute lists duties of every attorney, including to maintain respect for the courts, to counsel only actions that appear legal and just (except criminal defense), never to mislead judges by artifice or false statements, to preserve the client’s secrets “at any peril to himself,” to abstain from offensive personalities, and never to reject the cause of the defenseless or oppressed for personal reasons. The statute’s confidentiality wording has no exceptions on its face; how it interacts with Rule 1.6 was not checked.","c":"5 O.S. § 3","v":true},{"t":"Fee-sharing with litigation funders points to Rules 1.5 and 5.4","d":"The 2025 litigation-funding amendments say fee sharing by lawyers or firms must follow the Rules of Professional Conduct, naming Rules 1.5 and 5.4, and lawyer-funder agreements are outside the discoverable “commercial litigation funding agreement” definition. Funding agreements between a funder and a party must be produced on request with a sworn certification about foreign-state funding sources.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 3226(B)(1)(c), 3226.2","v":true}],"style":"State of Oklahoma","home":"https://www.oscn.net/","unique":{"mines":[{"t":"180-day service deadline with automatic dismissal","d":"If a defendant is not served within 180 days after the petition is filed and good cause is not shown, the action is deemed dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant, and the court must enter a dismissal order within 200 days if no defendant was served. Refiling depends on the savings statute, 12 O.S. § 100. Relation back of a new defendant also turns on notice within the 180 days.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2004(I), 2015(C)(3)"},{"t":"Don’t plead a dollar figure above the diversity amount","d":"In non-contract cases seeking more than the 28 U.S.C. § 1332 amount, the petition must say only that damages exceed that amount; below it, state the exact amount. Punitive-damage petitions may not state a figure. Defendants can file a Motion to Clarify Damages before the pretrial order to test a below-threshold pleading for removal purposes.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2008(A), 2009(G)-(H)"},{"t":"A “reservation of time” waives your Rule 12-type defenses","d":"The answer is due 20 days after service. Filing a reservation of time buys 20 more days but waives personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service, failure to state a claim, and capacity-to-be-sued defenses. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim that does not separately list each defect is denied without a hearing.","c":"12 O.S. § 2012(A)(1), (B)"},{"t":"There is no notice of appeal","d":"An appeal is commenced only by a petition in error filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after the written, signed judgment is filed. Filing something in the trial court does not commence an appeal, and a minute entry, verdict, or letter ruling never starts or satisfies the clock.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 990A(A), 696.2(D)"},{"t":"Post-trial motions toll only if filed within 10 days","d":"A motion for new trial, JNOV, or to vacate or reconsider suspends the appeal time only if filed within 10 days after the judgment is filed; a motion to vacate filed on day 11 through 30 is allowed but does not extend the 30-day appeal clock. Fee and cost motions never do.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 990.2(A)-(B), (D), 1031.1(B)"},{"t":"Interlocutory appeals by right need the record within 30 days too","d":"For orders on injunctions, receivers, class certification, attachment, or granting a new trial, the petition in error and the record must both be filed with the Supreme Court within 30 days after the order is filed.","c":"12 O.S. § 993(A)"},{"t":"Discovery response clocks wait for the answer","d":"Interrogatories, document requests, and requests for admission may be served any time after the petition is filed, but the 30-day response period does not start until an answer to the petition is filed, unless the court orders otherwise or the parties agree in writing.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 3233(A), 3234(B)(2)(a), 3236(A)"},{"t":"30-question caps and a 6-hour, business-hours deposition","d":"Interrogatories and requests for admission are each capped at 30 per party (subparts count separately for interrogatories). Depositions are limited to 6 hours between 8 a.m. and 5 p.m. on non-holiday weekdays unless agreed or ordered, and a nonparty need only attend in the county of residence, an adjoining county, or where served.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 3230(A)(3), (B), 3233(A), 3236(A)"},{"t":"Expert depositions are usable at trial for any purpose","d":"Any expert witness’s deposition may be used at trial for any purpose without showing the expert is unavailable, and a witness who simply lives outside the county qualifies as unavailable — far looser than FRCP 32(a)(4).","c":"12 O.S. § 3232(A)(3)(b), (e)"},{"t":"Rule 11 motions here can bring mandatory fees","d":"Monetary sanctions, including the movant’s attorney fees, are mandatory for a paper presented for an improper purpose, and in non-contract cases the court must order fees against a party whose claim or defense it finds frivolous or knowingly false at the dismissal, summary-judgment, or merits stage.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2011(C)(2)(c), 2011.1"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP motion: 60 days from service, and it freezes discovery","d":"Defendants sued over speech, petitioning, or association must move under the Citizens Participation Act within 60 days after service; filing suspends discovery, and a win carries mandatory fees and sanctions. Plaintiffs need clear and specific evidence of every element.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1432, 1434, 1438"}],"judge":[{"t":"No peremptory challenge in the statutes; disqualification for cause","d":"Title 20 provides no peremptory change of judge. A judge may not sit, without the parties’ consent of record, where the judge is interested, is related to a party within the fourth degree, was counsel, or the validity of the judge’s own prior work as counsel is at issue. In contested civil cases a judge may not sit if related within the third degree to any attorney of record, which includes that attorney’s associates and partners, absent consent of the appearing parties. Common-law grounds survive.","c":"20 O.S. § 1401","v":true},{"t":"Procedure: written application, then mandamus","d":"A party files a written application with the court clerk stating the grounds and asks the judge to certify the disqualification; if the judge does not do so at least 3 days before the trial setting, the party may seek mandamus from the proper tribunal. District Court Rule 15 (“Disqualification or Recusal of Judges in Civil and Criminal Cases”) supplies the detailed steps and time limits, but its text was not collected.","c":"20 O.S. § 1403; Rule 15, Rules for District Courts (not collected)","v":true},{"t":"Appellate judges","d":"A Justice who presided at trial may not participate; if fewer than seven qualified Justices remain, the Governor assigns a retired Justice or a qualified member of the Bar. A Court of Civil Appeals judge who presided at trial or was counsel may not participate.","c":"20 O.S. §§ 1402(A)-(B), 30.3","v":true}],"speech":[{"t":"Not established from collected text","d":"No Oklahoma constitutional text or case law on free speech was collected, so no claim is made that Oklahoma protects speech more broadly than the First Amendment. The Citizens Participation Act (12 O.S. §§ 1430-1440) is the main procedural protection.","c":"Okla. Const. art. 2, § 22 (not collected)","v":false}],"other":[{"t":"Civil procedure is statutory","d":"The Pleading Code and Discovery Code are statutes enacted by the Legislature, with section captions part of the act. Expect statutory amendments every session (for example, the 2025 litigation-funding discovery changes effective November 1, 2025), not court rule packages.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 2001, 3224","v":true},{"t":"Assignment of tort claims prohibited","d":"Claims not arising out of contract cannot be assigned, though subrogation is unaffected.","c":"12 O.S. § 2017(D)","v":true}]},"appeals":{"asof":"November 10, 2026","src":"Oklahoma statutes from the Oklahoma Legislature’s compiled titles (session laws effective through January 1, 2026): 12 O.S. §§ 653, 696.2, 696.3, 952, 990A, 990.1, 990.2, 991, 992, 993, 994, 995, 1031.1, 1437; 20 O.S. §§ 30.1-30.6, 30.14. The Oklahoma Supreme Court Rules (12 O.S. ch. 15, app. 1), which set the briefing schedule, brief limits, cross-appeal and counter-appeal deadlines, rehearing, and certiorari timing, were NOT collected: OSCN (oscn.net) began serving a Cloudflare Turnstile bot check during collection on November 10, 2026, and collection from that site stopped. Items drawn from those rules are omitted or flagged; verify them on OSCN before relying on this page.","intro":{"t":"Appeals in Oklahoma: one jurisdictional step, and the clock runs from a filed written judgment","d":"There is no notice of appeal. A civil appeal is commenced by filing a petition in error with the Clerk of the Supreme Court within 30 days after a written judgment, decree, or appealable order that meets 12 O.S. § 696.3 is filed with the trial court clerk. That filing is the only jurisdictional step; every other step in perfecting the appeal is not jurisdictional. All appeals go to the Supreme Court, which keeps some and assigns others to the Court of Civil Appeals; a Court of Civil Appeals decision can be reviewed only if a majority of the Supreme Court grants certiorari. Minute entries, verdicts, and letter rulings do not start the clock."},"groups":[["What starts the clock",[{"t":"Only a filed, signed, written judgment counts","d":"The filing of a written judgment, decree, or appealable order that conforms to § 696.3 and is signed by the court is a jurisdictional prerequisite to an appeal. A minute entry, a verdict, or an informal statement such as a letter announcing the ruling is not a judgment. Even where the ruling is enforceable when pronounced (for example, injunctions, divorce, probate, quiet title), the appeal time runs only from the filing of the written order.","c":"12 O.S. § 696.2(D)-(E)"},{"t":"Required contents of the judgment","d":"Caption, a statement of the disposition including the relief awarded, the liabilities imposed, and the amount of any prejudgment interest, and the court’s signature and title. It may include costs, fees, and post-judgment interest if already decided. The clerk endorses the filing date.","c":"12 O.S. § 696.3"},{"t":"Service within 3 days, and the late-mailing extension","d":"The party who prepared the judgment (or the court’s designee) must serve a file-stamped copy on all parties, including defaulted parties, within 3 days after filing and file a certificate of service. If the appellant did not prepare the judgment and the court record does not show mailing or service on the appellant within 3 days (excluding weekends and holidays), the 30 days run from the earliest date the record shows it was mailed or served.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 696.2(B), 990A(A)"}]],["Commencing the appeal",[{"t":"Petition in error in the Supreme Court within 30 days","d":"File the petition in error with the Clerk of the Supreme Court, not the trial court, within 30 days after the judgment is filed. Filing may be by delivery, or by certified or first-class mail, in which case the postmark (or other Postal Service proof of mailing) is the filing date; without that proof, the date the Clerk receives it controls.","c":"12 O.S. § 990A(A)-(B)"},{"t":"Only the petition in error is jurisdictional","d":"All other steps in perfecting the appeal are not jurisdictional, and parties may waive defects in perfecting an appeal except the timely petition in error (and a timely petition to review a certified interlocutory order). Defects that could have been raised in the trial court must be raised there promptly.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 990A(E), 992"},{"t":"Premature petitions can be saved","d":"A petition filed before the appeal time begins is dismissed as premature, but if the time begins before dismissal the appellant may file a supplemental petition in error without new costs. If the appeal is dismissed as premature, a new petition may be filed within the regular time or within 30 days after the dismissal notice is mailed, whichever is later.","c":"12 O.S. § 990A(F)"},{"t":"Designation of record needs the court reporter’s acknowledgment","d":"The district court clerk will not accept a designation of record unless it includes the court reporter’s signed acknowledgment of the transcript request (with date and deposit) or the attorney’s signed statement that no transcript was ordered and why. The same applies to counter-designations. The record must be complete within the time set by Supreme Court rule (not collected).","c":"12 O.S. § 990A(D), (G)"},{"t":"Whole case is before the Court; cross-appeals by rule","d":"A timely petition in error gives the Supreme Court jurisdiction of the entire action, and no further jurisdictional step is needed for any party to assert errors involving another party to the appeal. Time limits for counter-appeals and cross-appeals are set by Supreme Court rule; that rule was not collected, so check Okla. Sup. Ct. R. 1.25-1.27 on OSCN.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 990.1, 990A(C)"}]],["Post-trial motions",[{"t":"Motions filed within 10 days suspend the appeal","d":"A motion for new trial, for judgment notwithstanding the verdict, or to correct, open, modify, vacate, or reconsider, filed within 10 days after the judgment is filed, means no appeal may be commenced until an order disposing of it is filed. Then the appeal is due within 30 days after that order is filed, and the movant who lost may appeal the judgment and the ruling together in one appeal; successive appeals are not allowed.","c":"12 O.S. § 990.2(A)"},{"t":"Motions filed after 10 days do not extend anything","d":"A motion to correct, open, modify, vacate, or reconsider filed more than 10 days after the judgment does not extend the appeal time, and an appeal filed before it is not premature; the parties must tell the Supreme Court about the pending motion and its disposition. A motion for costs or attorney fees never extends the time to appeal.","c":"12 O.S. § 990.2(B), (D)"},{"t":"New trial: 10 days; vacatur by motion: 30 days","d":"A motion for new trial must be filed within 10 days after the conforming judgment is filed (later only by petition under § 655). The court may correct, open, modify, or vacate on motion filed within 30 days, or on its own within 30 days; after 30 days relief is by petition under § 1033 unless all appearing parties approve. Motions filed after the decision is announced but before the judgment is filed are deemed filed right after the judgment.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 653(A), (C), 1031.1(A)-(C)"},{"t":"A new-trial motion narrows the appeal","d":"A motion for new trial is not required to appeal, but if one is filed, no appeal may be taken until it is decided, and after denial the movant may not raise on appeal errors that were available but not included in the motion.","c":"12 O.S. § 991"}]],["Interlocutory and partial-judgment appeals",[{"t":"Interlocutory orders appealable by right","d":"Orders on attachments; granting (except ex parte), denying, or modifying injunctions; provisional remedies affecting substantial rights; appointing or refusing receivers; pendente lite payments; certifying or refusing to certify a class (with certain jurisdiction and exhaustion rulings reviewable only with a certification appeal); and orders granting a new trial or opening or vacating a judgment. The petition in error and the record must be filed with the Supreme Court within 30 days after the conforming order is filed.","c":"12 O.S. § 993(A)"},{"t":"Certified interlocutory orders","d":"Any other order affecting a substantial part of the merits may be reviewed if the trial judge certifies that an immediate appeal may materially advance the end of the litigation; the Supreme Court may decline, and if it accepts, states whether the trial court proceedings are stayed. The petition deadline is set by Supreme Court Rules 1.50-1.56 (not collected). Failing to take an available interlocutory appeal does not forfeit the error after final judgment.","c":"12 O.S. § 952(b)(3)"},{"t":"Judgment on fewer than all claims or parties","d":"Requires an express determination that there is no just reason for delay and an express direction to file a final judgment; otherwise the ruling is not final and remains revisable. The court may stay enforcement until later judgments are filed. Tracks FRCP 54(b).","c":"12 O.S. § 994"},{"t":"Anti-SLAPP rulings are expedited","d":"If the trial court does not rule on an Oklahoma Citizens Participation Act motion within 30 days after the hearing, it is denied by operation of law and the movant may appeal. Appellate courts must expedite any appeal or writ, interlocutory or not, from an order on the motion or the failure to rule.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1434(A), 1437"}]],["Court of Civil Appeals and certiorari",[{"t":"Assignment, panels, and precedent","d":"The Court of Civil Appeals decides only cases the Supreme Court assigns to it. It sits in at least four permanent three-judge divisions (two in Tulsa County, two in Oklahoma County), plus additional divisions the Supreme Court convenes; two judges must concur. Its opinions are not binding or citable as precedent unless approved for publication in the official reporter by a majority of the Supreme Court.","c":"20 O.S. §§ 30.1, 30.2, 30.5, 30.14"},{"t":"Certiorari to the Supreme Court","d":"A Court of Civil Appeals decision is reviewed only if a majority of the Justices grant certiorari, and the Supreme Court may recall a case. The petition for certiorari requires a $100 nonrefundable cost deposit (indigents may proceed under court rule). The deadline and form are in Supreme Court Rules 1.177-1.182, which were not collected.","c":"20 O.S. §§ 30.1, 30.4(B)"},{"t":"Frivolous appeals","d":"The Supreme Court or Court of Civil Appeals must dismiss a frivolous appeal, cross-appeal, or original proceeding and may sanction the appellant, counsel, or both, including reasonable expenses and attorney fees.","c":"12 O.S. § 995"}]],["Not collected: verify on OSCN",[{"t":"Briefing schedule, brief length, rehearing, record deadlines, e-filing","d":"These are governed by the Oklahoma Supreme Court Rules (12 O.S. ch. 15, app. 1), including Rules 1.10-1.11 (briefs), 1.13 (rehearing), 1.34 (time to complete the record), 1.36 (accelerated procedure for summary judgments and certain dismissals), and 1.4 (filings). The rule titles were seen on the OSCN index, but their text was not collected because OSCN began serving a bot check. No deadline or page or word limit is stated here for those items.","c":"Okla. Sup. Ct. R. 1.4, 1.10-1.13, 1.34, 1.36 (titles only, not text)"}]],["Deadlines",[{"t":"Petition in error (appeal from final judgment)","d":"30 days from filing of the written, signed judgment conforming to § 696.3 with the trial court clerk (or earliest record date of mailing to the appellant if not mailed within 3 days).\nJurisdictional. Filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court; mailing date by postmark counts.","c":"12 O.S. § 990A(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the written, signed judgment conforming to § 696.3 with the trial court clerk)","note":"Jurisdictional. Filed with the Clerk of the Supreme Court; mailing date by postmark counts."}},{"t":"Appeal after timely post-trial motion","d":"30 days from filing of the order disposing of a new-trial, JNOV, or correct/open/modify/vacate/reconsider motion filed within 10 days of judgment.\nNo appeal may be commenced while such a motion is pending. Fee and cost motions do not count.","c":"12 O.S. § 990.2(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the order disposing of a new-trial, JNOV, or correct/open/modify/vacate/reconsider motion filed within 10 days of judgment)","note":"No appeal may be commenced while such a motion is pending. Fee and cost motions do not count."}},{"t":"Motion for new trial","d":"10 days from filing of the conforming judgment.\nLater relief only by petition under § 655. Extended from the record mailing date if the judgment was not mailed within 3 days.","c":"12 O.S. § 653(A)","due":{"n":10,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the conforming judgment)","note":"Later relief only by petition under § 655. Extended from the record mailing date if the judgment was not mailed within 3 days."}},{"t":"Motion to correct, open, modify, or vacate","d":"30 days from filing of the conforming judgment.\nOnly a motion filed within 10 days suspends appeal time (§ 990.2). After 30 days, use a petition under § 1033.","c":"12 O.S. § 1031.1(B)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the conforming judgment)","note":"Only a motion filed within 10 days suspends appeal time (§ 990.2). After 30 days, use a petition under § 1033."}},{"t":"Interlocutory appeal by right (injunction, receiver, class certification, new trial granted, etc.)","d":"30 days from filing of the conforming order.\nPetition in error AND record due within the 30 days; the Supreme Court may extend time for the record for good cause.","c":"12 O.S. § 993(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the conforming order)","note":"Petition in error AND record due within the 30 days; the Supreme Court may extend time for the record for good cause."}},{"t":"New petition after dismissal as premature","d":"30 days from mailing of notice that the appeal was dismissed as premature (or the regular period, whichever is later).","c":"12 O.S. § 990A(F)(2)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (mailing of notice that the appeal was dismissed as premature)","note":""}},{"t":"Service of filed judgment on all parties","d":"3 days from filing of the judgment.\nDuty of the preparing party; a certificate of service must be filed.","c":"12 O.S. § 696.2(B)","due":{"n":3,"from":"the trigger date (filing of the judgment)","note":"Duty of the preparing party; a certificate of service must be filed."}},{"t":"OCPA ruling (else denied by operation of law, appealable)","d":"30 days from hearing on the anti-SLAPP motion.\nAppeal is expedited.","c":"12 O.S. §§ 1434(A), 1437(A)","due":{"n":30,"from":"the trigger date (hearing on the anti-SLAPP motion)","note":"Appeal is expedited."}}]]],"courts":[],"links":[["Official site","https://www.oscn.net/"]]},"rulesName":"Oklahoma Pleading Code","rulesSrc":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf","rulesAsof":"as checked November 10, 2026","rulesNote":"Each entry is our summary of the rule, not the rule itself. Tap Read the official text before you rely on it.","rules":[{"n":"2001","t":"Scope of the Oklahoma Pleading Code","s":"Governs procedure in the district courts in all civil suits, at law or in equity, unless a statute specifies a different procedure, and is construed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action. 12 O.S. §§ 1 through 2027 may be cited as the “Oklahoma Pleading Code,” and section captions are part of the act. Unlike the federal rules, the Code is a statute enacted by the Legislature, not a court rule.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2002","t":"One form of action","s":"There is one form of action, the “civil action.”","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2003","t":"Commencement of action","s":"A civil action is commenced by filing a petition (Oklahoma’s name for the complaint) with the court.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2003.1","t":"Commencement of actions by inmates","s":"Pro se inmate filings must be on court-approved forms supplied free by the clerk and must include 11 listed items, including a sworn list of every suit the inmate filed as an inmate in the previous 10 years and its disposition. A noncomplying filing is returned with the reasons and may not proceed; a knowing and willful failure to comply may lead to dismissal with prejudice. If a defendant pleads qualified or absolute immunity, the court may order a detailed reply.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2003.2","t":"Construction-related accessibility claims: pre-suit notice","s":"Before suing over a facility’s noncompliance with accessibility construction standards, the plaintiff must send the defendant written notice of the specific violations by certified mail, return receipt requested, at least 120 days before filing, and attach the notice and signed receipt to the petition. Missing documentation or a filing less than 120 days after notice requires dismissal without prejudice on the defendant’s motion. If the defendant fixes the defect before suit, the court dismisses and awards the defendant costs and reasonable attorney fees; the court may extend the time for a defendant making reasonable correction efforts.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2003.3","t":"Website accessibility claims: pre-suit notice","s":"Same scheme for claims that an organization’s website is not accessible to visually or hearing impaired users: certified-mail notice of the specific violations at least 120 days before filing, attached to the petition with the signed return receipt. Noncompliance means dismissal without prejudice on motion; a pre-suit fix means dismissal plus costs and attorney fees to the defendant, and sanctions if the action is frivolous under § 2011.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2004","t":"Process","s":"The clerk issues the summons on filing. Service by sheriff, licensed process server, or specially appointed person; individuals may be served personally, by abode service on a resident 15 or older, or on an authorized agent. Plaintiff may elect certified mail, return receipt requested, delivery restricted to the addressee (effective on receipt or refusal), but mail service supports a default only if the record shows acceptance or refusal, and after a refusal the plaintiff must mail a first-class copy and warning notice at least 10 days before seeking default. Publication (on a verified showing that due diligence cannot find the defendant) runs one day a week for 3 consecutive weeks, with an answer date at least 41 days after first publication; a publication default can be reopened within 3 years. A corporation without a findable registered agent may be served through the Secretary of State after listed service attempts and then has 40 days to answer. Court-ordered alternative service is allowed for individuals and entities after an affidavit of diligence. Oklahoma’s long-arm reaches any basis consistent with the state and federal constitutions. Key difference from FRCP 4(m): if service is not made within 180 days after filing and good cause is not shown, the action is deemed dismissed without prejudice as to that defendant (the savings statute, 12 O.S. § 100, applies to refiling), and the court must enter a dismissal order within 200 days if no defendant has been served.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2004.1","t":"Subpoena","s":"Subpoenas issue from the court where the action is pending and may be served anywhere in Oklahoma; the clerk issues blank signed subpoenas, and an Oklahoma-licensed attorney may also issue one. For out-of-state actions, the district court of the county where the deposition or production occurs issues the subpoena on proof of service of the deposition notice or document request. A plaintiff needs leave to serve a nonparty document subpoena within 30 days after service of summons on any defendant. Service is by delivery or certified mail restricted delivery by anyone 18 or older, with one day’s fees and mileage tendered if attendance is required; a copy of any pretrial document subpoena must be served on every party, and a nonparty production-only subpoena must set a date at least 7 days after service and carry the statutory warning not to produce until objections are resolved. Objections are due within 14 days after service (or before compliance if sooner), after which production requires a court order. Quash/modify grounds, ESI and privilege-log duties, and clawback track FRCP 45; disobedience may be contempt.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2004.2","t":"Notice of pendency of action (lis pendens)","s":"Filing the petition charges third persons with notice, but for real property notice is effective only after a notice of pendency identifying the case and legal description is filed with the county clerk. Notice of pendency has no effect unless service of process is made, or publication commenced, within 120 days after the petition is filed. Interests acquired or perfected after the notice is filed are void as against the prevailing party, except mechanics’ and materialmen’s liens.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2004.3","t":"Courier and overnight delivery in place of mail","s":"Where Title 12 requires or allows certified mail with restricted delivery, a party may instead use a commercial courier or overnight service with a signed written or electronic receipt; acceptance by a resident 15 or older at the abode counts. Courier may also replace ordinary mail. Deadlines are computed as if service were by mail. This section does not apply to filing with the court clerk.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2005","t":"Service and filing of pleadings and other papers","s":"Every later pleading, discovery paper, written motion (except ex parte), notice, appearance, demand, and offer of judgment must be served on each party; defaulted parties get new claims served like a summons and judgments served under § 696.2(B). Service on a represented party goes to the attorney, by hand, mail, third-party commercial carrier for delivery within 3 calendar days, or electronic means (fax or e-mail) only if the recipient consented in writing in that case, which can be done in the entry of appearance. Service by mail is complete on mailing, by carrier on delivery to the carrier, and electronically on transmission. Papers must be filed before service or within a reasonable time after and must state who was served and the date, place, and method. Fax or electronic filing is allowed only as permitted by a Supreme Court-approved rule, and the clerk may not refuse a paper solely for improper form. Difference from FRCP 5: electronic service requires written consent in the particular case.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2005.1","t":"Service of post-judgment motions in divorce actions","s":"All post-judgment motions in divorce proceedings must be served like a summons under § 2004(C).","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2005.2","t":"Entry of appearance; out-of-state counsel; withdrawal; address of record","s":"Every party must file an entry of appearance (by counsel or pro se) no later than its first filing; it must list name, signature, party represented, mailing address, phone and fax, OBA number, and firm, and may contain the written consent to electronic service. Filing it does not waive the § 2012(B) defenses. Out-of-state counsel’s motion must comply with Art. II § 5 of the Rules Creating and Controlling the Oklahoma Bar Association, with the required statement as an attached affidavit and the Oklahoma associate counsel’s entry of appearance filed with it. Withdrawal requires leave, a proposed order, service on the client, and the client’s signature or a certificate of notice; without successor counsel the motion must give the client’s name and address, and the order gives the client 30 days to file a new entry of appearance, after which a non-corporate party is deemed pro se. Counsel must keep a current address of record (including e-mail if consented); service there is valid for all purposes, including dismissal for failure to appear.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2006","t":"Time","s":"Exclude the trigger day and include the last day unless it is a legal holiday or a day the court clerk’s office is not open until its regular closing time. For periods under 11 days, intermediate holidays and closed days are excluded (except for times in §§ 765, 990.3, 1148.4, 1148.5, 1148.5A, and 1756). The court may enlarge a period on request before it expires, or after it expires for excusable neglect, but may not extend the time to appeal or to seek a new trial, judgment notwithstanding the verdict, or to correct, open, modify, vacate, or reconsider a judgment except as those sections allow. Written motions and notice of hearing must be served at least 5 days before the hearing, with supporting affidavits served with the motion. Add 3 days after service by mail, third-party commercial carrier, or electronic means; but a defendant served by mail answers within 20 days (or 35 days if elected under § 2012(A)) after receipt or refusal. Differences from FRCP 6: the under-11-days rule and the 5-day motion notice period.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2007","t":"Pleadings allowed; form of motions","s":"Allowed pleadings: petition and answer, reply to a counterclaim, answer to a cross-claim, third-party petition and third-party answer; the court may order a reply to an answer. Motions must be in writing (unless made at a hearing or trial), state the grounds with particularity, and state the relief sought. Demurrers, pleas, and exceptions for insufficiency of a pleading are abolished.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2008","t":"General rules of pleading","s":"A claim needs a short and plain statement showing entitlement to relief and a demand for judgment. Key difference: in non-contract cases seeking money damages above the federal diversity amount (28 U.S.C. § 1332), the pleading must not demand a specific sum and must state only that the damages exceed that amount; a claim at or below that amount must state the specific amount sought. Denials, the 20 listed affirmative defenses (including contributory negligence and injury by fellow servant), admission by failure to deny (except as to the amount of damages), and alternative or inconsistent pleading track FRCP 8.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2009","t":"Pleading special matters","s":"Capacity, fraud or mistake with particularity, conditions precedent, official documents, judgments, time and place, and special damage track FRCP 9. Oklahoma additions: a petition seeking punitive damages may not state a dollar amount but must say whether damages sought exceed the federal diversity amount; and when the plaintiff pleads less than that amount, the defendant may file a Motion to Clarify Damages before the pretrial order, requiring the plaintiff to show by a preponderance that any award will not exceed it, failing which the plaintiff must amend.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2010","t":"Form of pleadings","s":"Caption with court, title, file number, and designation; the petition names all parties, later pleadings may use the first party on each side. Numbered paragraphs, separate counts where helpful, adoption by reference, and written-instrument exhibits are part of the pleading. Tracks FRCP 10.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2011","t":"Signing of pleadings; sanctions","s":"Every paper must be signed by at least one attorney of record with the attorney’s Oklahoma Bar Association number (or by the unrepresented party), with address and phone. Signing certifies the same four points as FRCP 11(b). A sanctions motion must be separate and served, and it may not be filed unless the paper is not withdrawn or corrected within 21 days after service. Sanctions are limited to deterrence; no monetary sanctions against a represented party for legal contentions, and court-initiated monetary sanctions require a show-cause order before voluntary dismissal or settlement. Difference from FRCP 11: monetary sanctions are mandatory for an improper-purpose violation (paragraph 1) and must include the movant’s reasonable costs and attorney fees; “frivolous” is defined as knowingly asserted in bad faith or without any rational argument in law or fact. Does not apply to discovery under §§ 3226-3237.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2011.1","t":"Frivolous or knowingly false claims in non-contract actions: mandatory fees","s":"In any action not arising out of contract, when ruling on a motion to dismiss or summary judgment, or after adjudication on the merits, the court may determine whether a nonprevailing party’s claim or defense was frivolous or proven knowingly false (intent to deceive, by clear and convincing evidence). On that finding the court must order the nonprevailing party to reimburse the prevailing party’s reasonable costs and attorney fees for that claim or defense, and may add § 2011 sanctions. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2012","t":"Defenses and objections: when and how presented","s":"Answer within 20 days after service of summons and petition; a defendant may file a reservation of time extending the deadline 20 more days, but doing so waives personal jurisdiction, venue, process, service, failure-to-state-a-claim, and capacity-to-be-sued defenses (B(2)-(6), (9)). A cross-claim answer and a reply to a counterclaim are due in 20 days; the party requesting the summons or filing a counterclaim or cross-claim may elect a 35-day answer period. If a § 2012 motion or a summary judgment motion is denied or deferred, the responsive pleading is due 20 days after notice. Ten defenses may be raised by motion, including two not in FRCP 12(b): another action pending for the same claim, and lack of capacity to sue or be sued. A motion to dismiss for failure to state a claim must separately state each defect, or it is denied without a hearing. Motions to strike an insufficient defense are due within 20 days when no response is allowed. Waiver rules track FRCP 12(h) (with failure to state a claim also waivable, though a later no-relief-as-a-matter-of-law contention survives), an amended motion is allowed if sought at least 5 days before the hearing, and on dismissal the court must grant leave to amend if the defect can be cured and set a deadline, after which dismissal is with prejudice absent excusable neglect.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2013","t":"Counterclaim and cross-claim","s":"Compulsory and permissive counterclaims and cross-claims track FRCP 13, with contingent counterclaims allowed. Oklahoma limitations rules: a same-transaction counterclaim is not time-barred even if barred when the petition was filed and may support affirmative judgment; an unrelated money counterclaim that accrued before either claim was barred but is now barred may be used only to reduce the opposing claim; a counterclaim barred before the opposing claim arose cannot be used at all. An assignee (other than a holder in due course) takes subject to defenses and counterclaims, but only as a setoff. An omitted counterclaim not added by amendment within 20 days under § 2015(A) needs leave or written consent.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2014","t":"Third-party practice","s":"A defending party may serve a third-party petition on a nonparty who is or may be liable for all or part of the claim, or who is liable on a claim from the same transaction or occurrence; no leave is needed if filed within 10 days after serving the original answer (FRCP 14 allows 14 days). Third-party defendants’ defenses, claims against the plaintiff, and further impleader track FRCP 14; a plaintiff facing a counterclaim may implead likewise.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2015","t":"Amended and supplemental pleadings","s":"Amend once as a matter of course before a responsive pleading is served, or within 20 days after service if no response is permitted and the case is not on the trial calendar; omitted counterclaims and adding or dropping parties may be done as of course in the same window. Otherwise leave (with the proposed amendment attached to the motion) or written consent, freely given. Response due within the remaining time or 20 days after service of the amendment, whichever is longer. Amendments to conform to the evidence and supplemental pleadings track FRCP 15. Relation back of a party change requires notice and knowledge within the 180-day service period of § 2004(I) (not the FRCP 4(m) 90 days); an amendment adding an omitted counterclaim does not relate back.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2016","t":"Pretrial procedure","s":"Absent superseding legislation, pretrial conferences are governed by rules of the Oklahoma Supreme Court (see District Court Rule 5). Unlike FRCP 16, the statute itself contains no scheduling-order provisions.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2017","t":"Parties; capacity; ban on assignment of tort claims","s":"Real party in interest, capacity of any person or entity to sue and be sued, and representatives, next friends, and guardians ad litem for minors and incompetents track FRCP 17. Oklahoma addition: assignment of claims not arising out of contract is prohibited, though subrogation law is unaffected.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2018","t":"Joinder of claims and remedies; consolidation; separate trials","s":"Unlimited joinder of claims against an opposing party; a money claim may be joined with a claim to set aside a fraudulent conveyance without first getting judgment. Consolidation of actions with common questions and separate trials of any claim or issue (preserving jury trial) are in this section, corresponding to FRCP 18, 42(a) and 42(b).","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2019","t":"Joinder of persons needed for just adjudication","s":"Required-party joinder and the four equity-and-good-conscience factors for proceeding or dismissing when joinder is not feasible track FRCP 19. A pleading must name known required persons not joined and say why. Subject to the class-action section.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2020","t":"Permissive joinder of parties","s":"Plaintiffs or defendants may be joined if claims arise from the same transaction or occurrence, from a series of transactions with a common question, or — broader than FRCP 20 — if the claims are connected with the subject matter of the action. In quiet title and lien-foreclosure cases anyone claiming an interest in the property may be joined. The court may order separate trials and must consider whether joinder provides a fair and convenient forum for all parties.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2021","t":"Misjoinder and nonjoinder","s":"Misjoinder is not grounds for dismissal; parties may be added or dropped and claims severed at any stage on just terms, considering whether the result is a fair and convenient forum for all parties.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2022","t":"Interpleader","s":"A stakeholder exposed to double or multiple liability may join and require claimants to interplead, even if claims are not of common origin, and a defendant may interplead by cross-claim or counterclaim. Applies to suits against a sheriff over property taken on execution. The court may order the fund deposited and a bond; a disinterested stakeholder who deposits the property should be discharged with costs and, in the court’s discretion, a reasonable attorney fee.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2023","t":"Class actions","s":"Prerequisites and the three maintainable types track FRCP 23(a)-(b), but the petition must also contain factual allegations showing a plausible claim for relief. Certification is decided as soon as practicable, by an order defining the class and claims and appointing class counsel. Key differences: the certification order is reviewed de novo on appeal, and while that appeal is pending class discovery is stayed (the trial court keeps jurisdiction to implement a settlement); for actions filed after November 1, 2011, unless the defendant agrees, class membership is limited to Oklahoma residents and nonresidents who own relevant Oklahoma property or whose claims arise significantly from Oklahoma conduct; members not given individual notice in a (B)(3) class may opt out any time before liability is decided, and filing an individual suit counts as opting out. Settlement approval, objectors, and class counsel track FRCP 23(e) and (g). Fee awards require an evidentiary hearing with the court acting as fiduciary for the class, 13 listed factors, optional independent counsel or referee, and coupon-proportional fees when relief is in coupons.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2023.1","t":"Derivative actions by shareholders","s":"The petition must be verified, allege contemporaneous ownership (or devolution by operation of law), and state with particularity the demand efforts or reasons for not making them. The plaintiff must fairly and adequately represent similarly situated holders, and dismissal or compromise requires court approval and notice. Tracks FRCP 23.1.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2023.2","t":"Actions relating to unincorporated associations","s":"Members of an unincorporated association may sue or be sued as a class through representative members only if they will fairly and adequately protect the association and its members; § 2023(D) and (E) orders and settlement procedures apply. Tracks FRCP 23.2.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2024","t":"Intervention","s":"Intervention of right and permissive intervention track FRCP 24, with a rebuttable presumption that a guardianship petition impairs the interests of persons entitled to notice under 30 O.S. § 3-110. The motion must be served with the intervenor’s pleading; after it is granted, existing parties have 20 days to respond unless the court sets a shorter time. When a state statute’s constitutionality is challenged in a case where the State is not a party, the court must certify that to the Attorney General and allow the State to intervene, and the Attorney General must send the proceeding to the Speaker of the House and President Pro Tempore of the Senate, who may intervene.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2025","t":"Substitution of parties","s":"On a party’s death, any party or the decedent’s former attorney of record may file and serve a statement of death (Form 22) with proof of death. A motion for substitution must be made within 90 days after service of the statement, or the action is dismissed without prejudice as to the deceased party; nonparties are served with the motion like a summons. Incompetency, transfer of interest, and automatic substitution of public officers track FRCP 25. Difference: FRCP 25 does not require proof of death and allows a court-ordered extension framework under Rule 6(b); the Oklahoma text states the dismissal as mandatory.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2025.1","t":"Parent’s assignment to minor child of claim for child’s injury","s":"Parents entitled to recover damages for injury to a minor child may assign that claim to the child, and are deemed to have done so when they sue as guardian, guardian ad litem, or next friend and seek those damages for the child. Recoveries are handled under 12 O.S. § 83.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2026","t":"Forms","s":"The forms in § 2027 are sufficient under the Code and illustrate the simplicity and brevity it contemplates.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"2027","t":"Appendix of forms","s":"Twenty-two forms, including the summons (Form 1), petitions on a note, account, goods sold, money lent or paid by mistake, negligence, conversion, specific performance, fraudulent conveyance, and FELA (Forms 2-13), a motion to dismiss and answers presenting § 2012(B) defenses (Forms 14-16), third-party summons and motion (Forms 17-18), motion to intervene (Form 19), allegation of reason for omitting a party (Form 21), and the suggestion of death used under § 2025 (Form 22). Several forms still carry dates from the 1980s; check the current statute before relying on any form’s answer period or damage language.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3224","t":"Oklahoma Discovery Code: short title and scope","s":"§§ 3224 through 3237 are the Oklahoma Discovery Code, which governs discovery in all civil suits in all courts of the state.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3225","t":"Construction","s":"The Discovery Code is construed, administered, and employed to secure the just, speedy, and inexpensive determination of every action.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3225.1","t":"Discovery master","s":"On motion or its own initiative, after a hearing unless waived, the court may appoint a discovery master for consented duties or, with findings of necessity, cost-benefit, and no undue burden on court access, to resolve pretrial and post-trial discovery matters. The master must disclose conflicts within 14 days of appointment, and a motion to disqualify is due within 14 days of the disclosure, with review by the assigned judge (request within 5 days of refusal), then the presiding judge, then mandamus in the Supreme Court (within 5 days). Objections to or motions to adopt or modify the master’s order or report are due 14 days after it is filed, responses 15 days after that; fact findings and legal conclusions are reviewed de novo unless the parties stipulate otherwise, procedural rulings for abuse of discretion. Compensation comes from the parties or the fund, never the court fund. Compare FRCP 53.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3226","t":"General provisions governing discovery","s":"Methods include the usual devices plus authorizations for release of records. Initial disclosures are narrower than FRCP 26(a)(1): only a damages computation with supporting documents and, in injury cases, medical (and when relevant employment and school) record authorizations, due within 60 days after service unless stipulated, ordered, or objected to by motion; the same eight categories of cases are exempt. Scope: nonprivileged matter relevant to any party’s claim or defense, “reasonably calculated to lead to the discovery of admissible evidence,” and proportional to the needs of the case (Oklahoma kept the reasonably-calculated phrase the 2015 federal amendments dropped). Insurance agreements and, since November 1, 2025, commercial litigation funding agreements must be produced on request, the latter with a sworn certification on foreign-state funding sources, updated within 30 days if it becomes inaccurate. An officer, director, managing agent, or government official without knowledge may serve an affidavit naming a knowledgeable person (the deposition may still proceed subject to a protective order). Expert discovery is by interrogatory and deposition rather than FRCP 26(a)(2) reports; the expert interrogatory (opinions, qualifications, 10 years of publications, compensation, 4 years of testimony) counts as one interrogatory. Protective orders need an in-person or phone meet-and-confer certification; orders removing material from the public record need specific findings and sealed-envelope or e-filing sealing procedures, and a party planning to seek one must file under a pseudonym. Supplementation is narrower than FRCP 26(e). Any party may compel a discovery conference by a motion with a proposed plan; objections due 10 days after service. Every request, response, and objection must be signed, and an improper certification requires sanctions.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3226.1","t":"Abusive discovery; protective orders and expenses","s":"In addition to § 3226(C) orders, the court may issue a protective order authorizing or denying discovery on motion with a good-faith in-person or telephone conferral certification, on a finding that justice requires protection from annoyance, harassment, embarrassment, oppression, or undue delay, burden, or expense. Expenses, including attorney fees, may be shifted to the losing side after an opportunity for hearing unless its position was substantially justified or an award would be unjust, and may be apportioned on a split ruling.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3226.2","t":"Commercial litigation funding definitions","s":"Defines “commercial litigation funder” (anyone other than a contingent-fee attorney with a right to compensation contingent on and sourced from the proceeds of a civil action) and “commercial litigation funding agreement,” excluding attorney contingency fees and cost advances, agreements between lawyers or firms and funders (fee sharing remains governed by Rules of Professional Conduct 1.5 and 5.4), and consumer litigation funding under 14A O.S. § 3-801. Effective November 1, 2025. No federal counterpart.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3226A","t":"Withdrawal of discovery materials after judgment","s":"No less than 30 nor more than 60 days after a judgment or final order is filed when no appeal is taken, or within 30 days after the appellate mandate issues, the party or counsel must withdraw, on receipt to the clerk, filed discovery items not introduced into evidence, not in the appellate record, or not needed on remand.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3227","t":"Depositions before action or pending appeal","s":"A verified petition to perpetuate testimony is filed in the district court of the county where any expected adverse party resides, with notice served like a summons at least 20 days before the hearing (publication and appointed counsel for those not personally served; guardians ad litem for minors and incompetents). The court may order depositions if perpetuation may prevent a failure or delay of justice. Depositions pending appeal (or before appeal time runs) are allowed on motion in the district court. Such depositions are not filed unless ordered or needed, the taking attorney pays costs unless ordered otherwise, and similar out-of-state perpetuation depositions are admissible. Tracks FRCP 27.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3228","t":"Persons before whom depositions may be taken","s":"Within Oklahoma, depositions must be taken before a certified or licensed shorthand reporter (CSR or LSR), subject to a grandfather clause for pre-1990 steno-mask reporters certified to the Board of Examiners of Official Shorthand Reporters. Outside Oklahoma: before a person authorized by local or Oklahoma law, a court-commissioned person, or by letter rogatory. No deposition before a relative, employee, or attorney of a party or of counsel, or anyone financially interested. Difference from FRCP 28: the in-state CSR/LSR requirement.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3229","t":"Stipulations regarding discovery procedure","s":"Unless the court orders otherwise, parties may by written stipulation change who takes depositions and when, where, and how, and modify other discovery procedures, but a stipulation extending response times under §§ 3226, 3233, 3234, and 3236 needs court approval if it would interfere with a time set for completing discovery. Tracks FRCP 29.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3230","t":"Depositions upon oral examination","s":"No leave needed except for a prisoner, a person already deposed, or a deposition within 30 days after service of summons on any defendant (unless the notice certifies the witness is leaving the state); a request for leave must certify an in-person or phone attempt to get a stipulation. Unless agreed or ordered, a deposition may not exceed 6 hours (FRCP 30 allows 7) and must be taken between 8:00 a.m. and 5:00 p.m. on a non-holiday weekday. A nonparty witness need attend only in the county of residence, an adjoining county, or the county where served; a party may also be deposed in the county where the case is pending or where the party is when noticed. Notice must allow travel time plus 3 days for preparation. Nonstenographic and remote depositions require a stipulation or order (freely granted); organizational (corporate representative) depositions track FRCP 30(b)(6). Objections must be concise, nonargumentative, and nonsuggestive; instructions not to answer only to preserve privilege, enforce a court limit, or move to terminate or for protection; obstruction may be sanctioned with costs and fees. The deponent has 30 days after notice that the transcript is available to review and sign changes. Depositions are not filed but sealed and sent to the noticing attorney, who bears all expenses and must give each adverse party one free copy (including any video). Party deponents get no fees; transcription costs are taxable.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3231","t":"Depositions upon written questions","s":"Any party may depose any person on written questions served with a notice naming the witness and officer; prisoners only by leave. Cross questions within 30 days after service, redirect within 10 days after cross, recross within 10 days after redirect (FRCP 31 uses 14, 7, and 7 days). The officer takes the answers and handles the record under § 3230, and the taking party gives notice of filing. Costs taxed under § 3230(J).","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3232","t":"Use of depositions in court proceedings","s":"Deposition testimony is usable against parties present, represented, or noticed, to the extent the Oklahoma Evidence Code would admit it. Party and organizational-designee depositions may be used for any purpose. A witness’s deposition may be used for any purpose if the witness is dead; does not reside in the county where the case is pending or is absent from it (FRCP 32 uses 100 miles); is unable to testify; cannot be subpoenaed; or is an expert — a notable Oklahoma difference, since any expert’s deposition may be used without showing unavailability; or on exceptional circumstances. Rule of completeness, use after substitution, and use in a later action on the same subject between the same parties track FRCP 32. Nonstenographic offers need a transcript of the portions offered. Waiver of errors in notice, officer qualification, form, and completion tracks FRCP 32(d), with objections to the form of written questions due within the next question period or 5 days after the last questions.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3233","t":"Interrogatories to parties","s":"Limit of 30 interrogatories per party; an interrogatory asking for witness names and locations, or the existence, location, and custodian of documents, counts as one, but subparts count separately (FRCP 33 allows 25 including discrete subparts). More require a written stipulation or a motion showing good-faith conferral, good cause, and the proposed questions. Answer each separately under oath, restating the interrogatory before the answer. Interrogatories may be served without leave any time after the petition is filed, and answers are due 30 days after service, but the 30 days do not start until an answer to the petition is filed unless the court orders or the parties agree in writing. Objections must be specific or are waived. Contention interrogatories and the business-records option track FRCP 33.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3234","t":"Production of documents and things; entry on land","s":"Requests for documents, ESI, tangible things, and entry onto land track FRCP 34. Requests may be served without leave after the petition is filed; the written response is due within 30 days after service, but that period does not start until an answer to the petition is filed (unless ordered or agreed in writing). Objections must be specific, say whether materials are being withheld, and the response must state ESI form; production must be completed by the inspection date or another reasonable time stated. Nonparties are reached by subpoena under § 2004.1.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3235","t":"Physical and mental examinations","s":"Two tracks. When a party relies on its own physical (including blood group) or mental condition as an element of a claim or defense, an adverse party may obtain an examination by serving a request (no earlier than 30 days after service of summons, setting the exam at least 5 days after service) without a court order; the person examined must move to object, and bears the burden. Otherwise an examination by a suitably licensed or certified examiner requires a motion showing good cause, as under FRCP 35. A representative of the person examined may attend. Report exchange and privilege waiver track FRCP 35(b), including waiver of privilege as to other examiners of the same condition.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3236","t":"Requests for admission","s":"Limit of 30 requests per party (FRCP 36 has no numeric limit), with more allowed only by written stipulation or a motion showing conferral and good cause. May be served without leave after the petition is filed; matters are admitted unless answered or objected to within 30 days after service, but the 30 days do not start until an answer to the petition is filed (unless ordered or agreed in writing). Answer content, reasonable-inquiry duty, motion to determine sufficiency, conclusive effect, withdrawal, and use only in the pending action track FRCP 36.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"},{"n":"3237","t":"Failure to make or cooperate in discovery; sanctions","s":"Motions to compel go to the court where the action is pending or, for depositions, the court in the county where taken; the motion must certify an in-person or telephone good-faith conference. The court may order a privilege log listing author, recipients, date, length, nature or purpose, and basis, and may review documents in camera. Evasive or incomplete answers are failures to answer. Expense-shifting is mandatory after an opportunity for hearing unless substantially justified or unjust. Sanctions for disobeying orders, failure to attend one’s own deposition or to answer interrogatories or document requests, expenses for failure to admit, and failure to participate in a discovery plan track FRCP 37. Differences: Oklahoma keeps the older ESI safe harbor — absent exceptional circumstances no sanctions for ESI lost through routine, good-faith operation of an electronic information system — instead of the federal Rule 37(e) framework; and the requesting party pays the reasonable cost of making property available for inspection, which the court may tax or apportion.","u":"https://www.oklegislature.gov/OK_Statutes/CompleteTitles/os12.pdf"}]});
